Complete save states for Namco System 12 (Tekken 3), for rollback netplay and lockstep
handovers: a machine that loads a state, once or several times in a row, goes on exactly as the
one that saved it, and its state right after the load is the saver's, byte for byte. Plus Tekken
3's clock, which read 2 Feb. 2030.

- device_execute_interface: a CPU's pending input line events (the queue set_input_line fills and
  a synchronize() timer empties) weren't in states, and a load drops the scheduler's temporary
  timers, that one too, but kept the queue. A machine that loaded twice without running a frame in
  between (bench.mjs's replay does; so does any second load before the next retro_run) was left
  with an event no timer would apply, and every later change of that line queued behind it:
  Tekken 3's R3000 went its own way within the first frame (a random word at 0x8021F838 in RAM,
  registers, DMA timing). The queue is saved now and its timer armed again after a load. This is
  MAME's core: every CPU's states grow by 67 lines x 132 bytes (System 23 included).
- psxirq: its post_load set the CPU's interrupt line again, though the line is in the state (the
  CPU's input state and CAUSE): that queued the event above, and a timer the saving machine never
  had, on every load.
- psxcpu: the COM_DELAY register (0x1f801020) wasn't saved (found reading the code).
- PSX CPU, GTE, DMA, MDEC and GPU: registers and buffers no reset clears (R3000 and GTE
  registers, the scratchpad, DMA channel registers, the MDEC's tables, the GPU's packet) started
  with whatever the heap held where the device was built. They are in states, so a loaded state
  overwrote them, but two machines booted apart were different from the start, and Tekken 3 reads
  some of them: two Node runs whose only difference was the script's file name (so the length of
  a string in the environment, so where everything landed) were apart by frame 1200. Zero now.
- scheduler: presave numbers the active timers (their order when a state is loaded); an inactive
  one kept the number it had when last saved active, so two identical machines' states differed
  by when each had last saved (Tekken 3's EEPROM write timer, under rollback). Inactive ones are 0.
- H8 SCI: m_clock_step started as whatever the heap held (it is saved, and only read once a
  clock runs, which sets it): zero, so two machines' states match from power-on.
- MIPS III (System 23's R4650, found checking Time Crisis II): the interpreter's FPU condition
  flags (m_cf; the recompiler keeps them in FCR31) weren't saved. A machine that loaded a state
  had its own, which CFC1 merges into FCR31 and BC1T/BC1F branch on: in a game, Time Crisis II
  wrote a different condition bit back into FCR31 within 120 frames of a load.
- Lua: its idle timer was re-armed after every load (enabled, expiring never), unlike in the
  machine that saved: now it is turned off, as there are no tasks waiting. It never fired; only
  the state's bytes differed.
- H8 SCI, Tekken 3's clock (not a save-state problem): in clocked synchronous mode the clock
  output idles high once a transfer stops. System 12's BIOS stops receiving in the middle of a
  byte after reading its RTC4543, which left the clock low, so the next read began with a rising
  edge and sampled a bit before the chip had sent one: every other read came a bit late, and the
  first-boot screen said "2 Feb. 2030" for patch 0003's 1998-01-01. It now says
  "Thu. 1 Jan. 1998".

